Esc remains an overall exit, discarding pending tags. The wrapper signals
completion before keeping the pane alive. Waiting for that signal synchronizes
picker completion before querying c (still 1) and z (absent).

    :t new-session -d -s exit-ui "smth --no-base; tmux wait-for -S tags-exited; cat" (success)
    :t resize-window -t exit-ui:0 -x 100 -y 10 (success)
    :p exit-ui:0.0
    :settle -d 2s (settled)
    :k target C-t Z esc
    :t wait-for tags-exited (success)
    :t show-options -qv -t '=target:' @smth.tag.c (success)

```
1
```

    :t show-options -qv -t '=target:' @smth.tag.z (success)

A captured target disappearing before confirmation must report failure, not
redirect the edit to another session. The wrapper signals the failed picker's
exit; its captured error explains that previously applied changes may persist.
Before closing the target, the snapshot shows z staged and the apply control
visible because uppercase Z starts batch editing without committing.

    :t new-session -d -s failure-ui "smth --no-base; tmux wait-for -S tags-failed; cat" (success)
    :t resize-window -t failure-ui:0 -x 100 -y 10 (success)
    :p failure-ui:0.0
    :settle -d 2s (settled)
    :k target C-t Z
    :snap -e 'tags: acz \[C-t\] accept'

```terminal
session: target
  1/5 | [C-s] ↑l ↑r ↓n | [C-r] repo: none
▌■ target                               ac                                                         ┃
                                                                 tags: acz [C-t] accept [C-g] cancel
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────





```

    :$ smth --no-base --close target (exit: 0)
    :k C-t
    :t wait-for tags-failed (success)
    :snap -e 'failed to apply tag z'

